[QA 0] The paper proposes an onset detection algorithm combining short-time spectral average-based onset strength signal estimation, chirp group delay-based smoothing, and valley-peak distance-based peak picking, which achieves competitive performance with state-of-the-art methods while being computationally more efficient, especially for instruments with fast attack transients. | The key contribution of the paper "Chirp Group Delay based Onset Detection in Instruments with Fast Attack" is proposing an onset detection algorithm that combines short-time spectral average for onset strength signal estimation, chirp group delay-based smoothening, and valley-peak distance-based peak picking, which performs on par with state-of-the-art methods like superflux and CNN-based onset detection, while being computationally more efficient.
[QA 1] The paper calculates the transverse momentum-dependent parton distributions (TMDs) of the $\rho$ meson using light-front wave functions within the Nambu–Jona-Lasinio model, revealing typical transverse momenta of quark TMDs and demonstrating excellent adherence to positive constraints while investigating spin densities in transverse momentum space. | The key contribution of this paper is deriving the transverse momentum dependent parton distributions (TMDs) of the ρ meson from the overlap representations of its light-front wave functions calculated within the Nambu–Jona-Lasinio model.
[QA 2] The paper introduces a comprehensive 5-component topology-preserving Hodge decomposition for vector fields on domains embedded in regular Cartesian grids, enabling efficient and accurate decomposition while maintaining pairwise orthogonality and topological consistency. | The key contribution of this paper is introducing a comprehensive 5-component topology-preserving Hodge decomposition framework for vector fields on domains defined by level-set functions in Cartesian grids, without requiring explicit mesh generation, while maintaining orthogonality and accurately capturing the underlying topology.
[QA 3] The paper demonstrates a method for performing all-microwave spectroscopy, readout, and dynamic nuclear polarization of individual nuclear spins in a crystal using hyperfine coupling to Er³⁺ ions, enabling universal single-nuclear-spin spectroscopy in various materials. | The key contribution of the paper "All-microwave readout, spectroscopy, and dynamic polarization of individual nuclear spins in a crystal" is that it demonstrates the ability to perform readout, spectroscopy, and dynamic nuclear polarization of individual 183W nuclear spins in a CaWO4 crystal via their hyperfine interaction with neighboring Er3+ ions, relying exclusively on microwave driving and detection at millikelvin temperatures.
[QA 4] The paper demonstrates that the chromatic quasisymmetric function of a labeled path graph is symmetric if and only if the labeling is the natural labeling or its reverse, providing a complete classification of such labelings for path graphs. | The key contribution of this paper is proving the following theorem:

The chromatic quasisymmetric function of a labeled path graph on n vertices is symmetric if and only if the labeling is the natural labeling 1, 2, ..., n or its reverse n, ..., 2, 1.

The proof uses ribbon diagrams to encode the ascent-descent patterns of labeled path graphs, and carefully analyzes certain subconfigurations in the ribbon diagrams that force the chromatic quasisymmetric function to be non-symmetric when the path labeling is not natural. The paper also shows that the star graph K_1,n-1 has a non-symmetric chromatic quasisymmetric function for all labelings when n≥4, and provides a sufficient condition for when the chromatic quasisymmetric function of a tree is not palindromic (and hence not symmetric).
