[QA 0] The paper refutes the previous approximation used to determine the critical strain tensor in magnetar crusts under magnetic stress, demonstrating that it overestimates the strain magnitude and failure timescale, and introduces a more accurate method based on solving differential equations to correctly assess crustal failure. | Here is a one sentence summary of the key contribution of the paper:

The paper evaluated the validity of an algebraic approximation used in previous studies for determining the elastic strain tensor due to magnetic stress in the crust of a neutron star, showing that the approximation does not represent the correct strain tensor, and stressing the need to revisit evolutionary calculations using a proper approach of solving the differential equations.
[QA 1] The paper provides an analytical framework for the taxicab distance based Best-Worst Method, revealing that in some cases it can lead to multiple optimal weight sets, offering a robust mathematical foundation that enhances understanding and efficiency of the model in multi-criteria decision-making. | The key contribution of this paper is developing an analytical framework for obtaining optimal weights in the taxicab distance based Best-Worst Method (BWM) for multi-criteria decision-making by solving the underlying optimization problem analytically.
[QA 2] The paper proposes a collaborative computing scheme that optimizes adaptive video streaming in satellite-terrestrial networks by decomposing the task offloading problem into path selection and resource allocation with adaptive bitrate control, thereby enhancing service performance metrics including quality of experience and task completion rate while minimizing service delay. | The key contribution of this paper is presenting a collaborative computing scheme that optimally allocates computing tasks to various nodes within satellite-terrestrial networks to enhance adaptive video streaming performance, including quality of experience (QoE), by leveraging the collaboration between ground devices/users and satellites.
[QA 3] The paper provides analytical expressions for the minimum strength of association required for unobserved covariates to reverse statistically insignificant regression results, revealing that such reversals are difficult to achieve through merely reducing standard errors and are more likely due to altering point estimates. | The key contribution of this paper is that it characterizes the minimum strength of association that an unobserved covariate needs to have with the dependent and independent variables in order to overturn a statistically insignificant regression result. The paper introduces analytical metrics called "robustness values for insignificance" that quantify how difficult it is for an unobserved covariate to turn an insignificant result into a significant one, either through changes in the point estimate, standard errors, or both. These metrics can be used to conduct sensitivity analyses and bound the extent of potential p-hacking due to inclusion of control variables.
[QA 4] The paper proposes a novel hierarchical control method combining adaptive model matching based on data-driven adaptive FRIT with model predictive control to achieve high-precision displacement control of water-hydraulic artificial muscles while considering input constraints and improving robustness against experimental data variability. | The key contribution of this paper is proposing a novel hierarchical data-driven adaptive model matching based model predictive control method for high-precision displacement control of water-hydraulic artificial muscles by combining adaptive fictitious reference iterative tuning (A-FRIT) and fictitious reference iterative tuning based model predictive control (FMPC) to achieve robustness against inappropriate initial experimental data while considering input constraints.
